    'nuances' => array(
        'European Portuguese mandatory contractions can never be left uncontracted, and inline HTML tags do not license breaking them. Translate the sentence as a whole, then place the tags: preposition + article ALWAYS fuses — em+a/as/o/os = na/nas/no/nos, de+a/o = da/do, a+a = à, por+o = pelo, em+um = num. Write "Apoiando-nos <a href=\'/history\'>nas nossas muitas marcas,</a>", never "Apoiando-nos em <a>as nossas muitas marcas,</a>". If the contraction would straddle an anchor boundary, move the article inside the anchor rather than splitting it. "em as", "de a", "a a" are impossible in Portuguese and are the single most recognisable machine-translation tell.',
        'Never leave a Brazilian variant anywhere in the file. Always write: maquinagem (not usinagem), contactar/Contacte-nos (not contatar/Contate-nos), ligado / em contacto (not conectado), insufladores or turbo-sopradores (not sopradores), equipa (not equipe), contacto (not contato), monitorização (not monitoramento), fiável (not confiável), ecrã (not tela), ficheiro (not arquivo), registo (not registro), receção (not recepção), fabrico (not manufatura). Check short nav labels and CTA buttons too — that is where the Brazilian form usually survives while the body copy is correct.',
        'One English string gets exactly one Portuguese rendering across the whole site. Before delivering, group the source strings you translated and diff the outputs: "precision machining" must not be both "usinagem" and "maquinagem"; "metals automation" must not be both "automação" and "automatização"; "aftermarket" must not be both "mercado de reposição" and "mercado secundário"; "purpose" must not be "missão", "propósito" and "Objetivo"; "powertrain" must not be "grupos motopropulsores" and "transmissão"; "hub" must not be "centro de recursos" and "o hub". Pick the term and repeat it verbatim, including in headings, sub-labels, footers and mobile duplicates.',
        'A sentence fragment and the full sentence that contains it must agree word-for-word. Sites store the same copy split across segments (a hero paragraph plus the anchor text inside it, a heading plus its coloured <span>). Translate the fragment so it is a literal substring of the full string, with the same wording, the same contraction and the same case, so the rendered page never shows two versions of one sentence.',
        'Do not strengthen, weaken or broaden a claim. Translate the verb that is written: "predict" is "preveja/prever", never "previna/prevenir" (prevent); "utilities construction" is "construção de redes de serviços públicos", never "construção de infraestruturas"; "powertrain" is "grupo motopropulsor", never "transmissão"; "steerable dyno" is "dinamómetro direcional", never a bare "de direção". Never add a word the source does not contain — "Test stands featuring Bauer" is "Bancos de ensaio com Bauer", not "com tecnologia Bauer". A product claim that grows in translation is a legal and commercial exposure.',
        'Keep the English superlative on the same adjective. "your toughest, most complex challenges" is "os seus desafios mais difíceis e complexos" — the strongest adjective stays first and "mais" governs the pair. Never demote the leading superlative to a plain adjective at the end of the list.',
        'Preserve the source punctuation logic in option and form labels. An English slash is either/or and stays a slash: "Machine Installation / Commissioning" is "Instalação / comissionamento de máquinas", never "Instalação e Comissionamento". A comma list stays a comma list; an "and" stays "e"; an "or" stays "ou".',
        'Use the imperative, not the bare infinitive, whenever the sentence addresses the reader. In running prose write "Abra um pedido de assistência", "Preencha o formulário", "Contacte-nos" — the bare infinitive ("Abrir um pedido…") only belongs on a standalone button label. A verb-initial infinitive inside a sentence reads as a UI string pasted into copy.',
        'Translate situational "where" as "em que", never the locative "onde". "Critical issues, where the machine is down" is "Problemas críticos, em que a máquina está parada". Reserve "onde" for actual physical places ("Onde se encontra a máquina?").',
        'Use the industrial register for downtime: a machine that has stopped producing is "parada", "imobilizada" or "avariada", never "inativa" (which means idle by choice or deactivated) and never "tempo de inatividade" (use "tempo de paragem"). This wording appears on emergency support tickets, so the wrong register undersells the severity.',
        'Strip English calques and pleonasms instead of transliterating them. "bring it all together" is "integramos tudo" or "juntamos todas as peças" — "reunir" already means "bring together", so "tudo junto" is redundant. "and counting" is "e a aumentar", not "e a contar". "Industries served" is "Setores que servimos", not "Indústrias servidas". "History and brands" is "História e marcas" — "histórico" means a log or track record, not a company’s history. "Off-highway & mobile" is "Fora-de-estrada e máquinas móveis".',
        'Portuguese cannot stack nouns the way English does; insert the preposition and inflect for number. "CMT-TR Polimer Rotor Balancing Machine" is "Máquina de equilibragem de rotores de polímero CMT-TR", never "Máquina de Equilibragem de Rotor Polímero". Keep the model code at the end and keep the card in sentence case so it matches its sibling cards.',
        'Put the definite article before a company or product name when it is the subject or the object of a sentence: "Como pode a Burke Porter apoiar…", "o Burke Porter Group", "da Ascential Care", "no Ascentialytics", "O Ascentialytics oferece-lhe…". Dropping the article reads as Brazilian and is inconsistent with the rest of the site.',
        'A singular sector adjective can never modify a plural noun. Rephrase with "nos setores": "your automotive and truck initiatives" is "as suas iniciativas nos setores automóvel e de camiões", never "as suas iniciativas automóvel e de camiões". When in doubt, insert a head noun rather than leaving an uninflected adjective.',
        'Never repeat the same head noun twice inside one noun phrase. "aerospace, automotive, and other diverse industries" is "as indústrias aeroespacial e automóvel e outros setores diversos" (or "…e de outros setores diversos") — swap the second occurrence for "setores"/"áreas" rather than echoing "indústrias".',
        'Login and account wording in pt-PT: "Log in" is "Iniciar sessão" (not "Entrar"/"Acessar"), "Log out" is "Terminar sessão", "Sign up" is "Registe-se" or "Crie a sua conta" (not "Cadastre-se"), "Contact us" is "Contacte-nos" (never "Fale conosco"/"Contate-nos"), "subscription" is "subscrição" (not "assinatura"), "billing" is "faturação" (not "faturamento"), "training" is "formação" (not "treinamento").',
        'Use sentence case in every Portuguese headline, including press-release headlines, card titles and priority labels: "Ascential Technologies promove Brunno Moretti a Diretor de Tecnologia", "Prioridade 3: Prioridade baixa / Rotina". Never carry English title case across, and never render the same headline title-cased in a nav card and sentence-cased in the body.',
        'Numbers follow European Portuguese conventions: the thousands separator is a space or a period and the comma is the decimal separator. Write "13 000" or "13.000" — never "13,000", which a Portuguese reader parses as thirteen. Never copy an English-formatted number through unchanged, including inside statistics and counters.',
        'Brand, product and division names stay exactly as written in English and are never translated or partially translated: Ascential, Ascential Care, Ascentialytics, Ascential Medical & Life Sciences, Sentia, Cimat, Galileo, Kleinknecht, Lismar, Titan, Burke Porter, WinterPark, XL Machine, Bauer, ADAS Garage, and the tagline "Impossible? Done.". Translate only the surrounding common noun ("divisão Medical & Life Sciences"). If a name is kept English in one place it must be kept English in every place.',
        'Reproduce the HTML markup of the source exactly, not the markup of the previous translation. If the English separates a brand span from its description — <span class="tmm-brand-word">Lismar</span> NDT inspection — the Portuguese must keep the same split: <span class="tmm-brand-word">Lismar</span> inspeção de END. Never extend a styling span to swallow words that were outside it in the source, and never merge, drop, reorder or re-attribute tags.',
        'VARIANT: this site\'s Portuguese is EUROPEAN PORTUGUESE (pt-PT). Use pt-PT spelling and vocabulary throughout and never mix with Brazilian forms: "equipa" not "equipe", "dinamómetro" not "dinamômetro", "peças sobressalentes" not "peças de reposição", "selecione a sua equipa", "contacto" not "contato", "registo" not "registro". Mixing the two is the single strongest machine-translation tell a Portuguese reader sees.',
        'Use the possessive with the definite article consistently: "a nossa plataforma", "os nossos técnicos", "a sua empresa". Never mix "nossa plataforma" and "a nossa plataforma" on one page.',
